/* Reviewed English edition for Phase 13, Sessions 05-08: lessons 1-35. */
window.AppNhoPhase13EN = {"lessons":{"1":{"title":"Garment Product Development Process","cat":"Links to Design and Product Development","overview":"The garment product development process must be understood as part of the wider product and operating system. Product development translates market requirements into design, materials, construction, inspection standards and manufacturing processes. Early material decisions affect cost, fit, durability, hand feel and the ability to manufacture at scale. In practice, learners must distinguish the input data, the decision to be made and the evidence required to validate that decision. Without all three, the work can remain at the level of a concept description rather than becoming a controlled process.","method":"Use a stage-gate process: brief → concept → preliminary material selection → prototype → testing → wear trial → pre-production → production approval. Each gate needs approval criteria, an authorized approver, controlled document revisions and a list of open risks. Before finalizing a result, verify that the data is complete, the relevant documents are consistent and the approval authority is clear. Record who performed the work, when it was updated, which document revision was used and which assumptions remain valid. If information is missing or contradictory, record it as an item requiring confirmation instead of making an unsupported assumption to complete the file.","caution":"A visually attractive sample produced in the development room is not proof that the product is ready for mass production. Avoid turning a single test result into a conclusion about the entire system. A correct calculation can still lead to a wrong decision when its input data is outdated, and a passing specimen does not necessarily represent every lot unless variation has been assessed. When requirements, materials, suppliers, operating conditions or document revisions change, determine the scope of impact before proceeding.","deep":"The development process should maintain a connected record set covering the brief, drawings, bill of materials (BOM), material samples, measurement charts, test results and approval records. At each stage gate, check that the design is compatible with manufacturing capability, including available machinery, curve radii, layer thickness, sewing tolerances, stretch direction and supply capacity. When a decision changes, update the technical pack, BOM, reference sample and test plan together. Production handover should take place only when the relevant revisions are aligned and every open issue has an assigned owner. Material selection should not begin with a fabric’s trade name. First describe the end-use environment, activity level, exposure to water or heat, laundering frequency, appearance requirements, expected service life and cost ceiling. Translate each requirement into an assessable property, select representative material samples and define suitable test methods. At every review, retain the reference sample, lot code, construction, composition, finish, test results and acceptance decision. If the supplier or construction changes, do not assume that previous results remain valid; reassess the properties that may be affected. For every result, record the test method, preconditioning conditions, sampling direction, number of specimens and units. Do not compare values obtained using different conditioning or test methods. With anisotropic materials, results in the machine and cross directions may differ substantially, so specimen orientation must remain consistent during both testing and interpretation. Laboratory results must be considered alongside the intended end use: a specimen that meets a mechanical requirement may still provide an unsuitable wearing experience or fail under actual laundering conditions. When a result is close to an acceptance limit, consider variation between specimens and lots rather than relying on the average alone. At a minimum, each practical assessment should retain source data, the decision made, the approver, supporting evidence and the next action if the result falls outside requirements. Effectiveness should be judged not merely by whether the task was completed, but by whether deviations were detected early, their causes could be traced and recurrence was prevented.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Garment Product Development Process\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Garment Product Development Process</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Garment Product Development Process</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Approval Workflow</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Brief</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Design</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Prototype</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Production Approval</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and related revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":1},"2":{"title":"Translating User Needs into Technical Requirements","cat":"Links to Design and Product Development","overview":"Translating user needs into technical requirements is part of the wider product and operating system. A brief often contains subjective terms such as lightweight, cool, durable, soft, waterproof or highly stretchable. These descriptions must be converted into measurable properties and suitable test methods. In practice, learners must distinguish the input data, the decision to be made and the evidence required to validate that decision. Without all three, the work can remain at the level of a concept description rather than becoming a controlled process.","method":"Define the conditions of use, body area, climate, number of wash cycles, abrasion exposure, loading, safety requirements and expected service life. For each need, record the property, unit, test method, specimen conditions, acceptance threshold and approval source. Before finalizing a result, verify that the data is complete, the relevant documents are consistent and the approval authority is clear. Record who performed the work, when it was updated, which document revision was used and which assumptions remain valid. If information is missing or contradictory, record it as an item requiring confirmation instead of making an unsupported assumption to complete the file.","caution":"Do not assign an arbitrary test threshold to subjective claims such as “durable” or “cool”. Link each criterion to the intended use and the buyer’s requirements. Avoid turning a single test result into a conclusion about the entire system. A correct calculation can still lead to a wrong decision when its input data is outdated, and a passing specimen does not necessarily represent every lot unless variation has been assessed. When requirements, materials, suppliers, operating conditions or document revisions change, determine the scope of impact before proceeding.","deep":"Convert a user need into a verifiable requirement by identifying the user, environment, duration of use, care method and the failure that would matter. Replace subjective adjectives with properties: “cool” may relate to air permeability, heat transfer, moisture management and skin-contact sensation; “durable” may involve tensile strength, tear strength, abrasion, seam performance or laundering. For each property, specify the evaluation method, specimen, conditions, acceptance criterion and approval source. Every product claim should be traceable to a test and a defined scope of application. Material selection should not begin with a fabric’s trade name. First describe the end-use environment, activity level, exposure to water or heat, laundering frequency, appearance requirements, expected service life and cost ceiling. Translate each requirement into an assessable property, select representative material samples and define suitable test methods. At every review, retain the reference sample, lot code, construction, composition, finish, test results and acceptance decision. If the supplier or construction changes, do not assume that previous results remain valid; reassess the properties that may be affected. For every result, record the test method, preconditioning conditions, sampling direction, number of specimens and units. Do not compare values obtained using different conditioning or test methods. With anisotropic materials, results in the machine and cross directions may differ substantially, so specimen orientation must remain consistent during both testing and interpretation. Laboratory results must be considered alongside the intended end use: a specimen that meets a mechanical requirement may still provide an unsuitable wearing experience or fail under actual laundering conditions. When a result is close to an acceptance limit, consider variation between specimens and lots rather than relying on the average alone. At a minimum, each practical assessment should retain source data, the decision made, the approver, supporting evidence and the next action if the result falls outside requirements. Effectiveness should be judged not merely by whether the task was completed, but by whether deviations were detected early, their causes could be traced and recurrence was prevented.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Translating User Needs into Technical Requirements\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Translating User Needs into Technical Requirements</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Translating User Needs into Technical Requirements</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Approval Workflow</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">User Need</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Property</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test Method</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Acceptance Criteria</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and related revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":2},"3":{"title":"Material Structure Analysis","cat":"Product Development and Material Selection","overview":"Material structure analysis must be considered as part of the wider product and operating system. Textile materials comprise fibers, yarns, woven, knitted or nonwoven structures, density, mass, finishes and bonded layers. Finished-product performance results from the interaction of the complete structure. In practice, learners must distinguish the input data, the decision to be made and the evidence required to validate that decision. Without all three, the work can remain at the level of a concept description rather than becoming a controlled process.","method":"Create a material map covering fiber composition, yarn structure, yarn count, weave or knit, GSM, thickness, density, finish, coating or lamination and intended use. Compare supplier specifications with incoming inspection results. Before finalizing a result, verify that the data is complete, the relevant documents are consistent and the approval authority is clear. Record who performed the work, when it was updated, which document revision was used and which assumptions remain valid. If information is missing or contradictory, record it as an item requiring confirmation instead of making an unsupported assumption to complete the file.","caution":"Do not infer overall performance from fiber composition or GSM alone. Avoid turning a single test result into a conclusion about the entire system. A correct calculation can still lead to a wrong decision when its input data is outdated, and a passing specimen does not necessarily represent every lot unless variation has been assessed. When requirements, materials, suppliers, operating conditions or document revisions change, determine the scope of impact before proceeding.","deep":"Analyze a material at several levels: fiber composition, yarn structure, woven, knitted or nonwoven construction, mass, finishing and multilayer architecture. Two fabrics with the same fiber content and GSM can differ in drape, breathability, strength, pilling and dimensional stability because of differences in density, yarn count, interlacing or surface treatment. When receiving a sample, record its code, face and reverse, machine and cross directions, lot and finish condition. Examine a cross-section or deconstruct the material when necessary to verify coatings, membranes and substrate layers. For every result, record the test method, preconditioning conditions, sampling direction, number of specimens and units. Do not compare values obtained using different conditioning or test methods. With anisotropic materials, results in the machine and cross directions may differ substantially, so specimen orientation must remain consistent during both testing and interpretation. Laboratory results must be considered alongside the intended end use: a specimen that meets a mechanical requirement may still provide an unsuitable wearing experience or fail under actual laundering conditions. When a result is close to an acceptance limit, consider variation between specimens and lots rather than relying on the average alone. At a minimum, each practical assessment should retain source data, the decision made, the approver, supporting evidence and the next action if the result falls outside requirements. Effectiveness should be judged not merely by whether the task was completed, but by whether deviations were detected early, their causes could be traced and recurrence was prevented.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Material Structure Analysis\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Material Structure Analysis</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Material Structure Analysis</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Approval Workflow</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Fiber Content</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Yarn</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Fabric Structure</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Finishing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and related revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":3},"4":{"title":"Selecting Materials for Their Intended Use","cat":"Product Development and Material Selection","overview":"Material selection must be based on the intended use and considered within the wider product and operating system. Fabrics for shirts, sportswear, protective clothing, outerwear and underwear face different use conditions. The same fiber can produce very different materials depending on construction and finishing. In practice, learners must distinguish the input data, the decision to be made and the evidence required to validate that decision. Without all three, the work can remain at the level of a concept description rather than becoming a controlled process.","method":"Build a product-specific requirements matrix covering drape, air permeability, moisture management, tensile and tear strength, abrasion resistance, dimensional stability, hand feel, color, care requirements and cost. Score alternatives using agreed weightings, then test the leading options. Before finalizing a result, verify that the data is complete, the relevant documents are consistent and the approval authority is clear. Record who performed the work, when it was updated, which document revision was used and which assumptions remain valid. If information is missing or contradictory, record it as an item requiring confirmation instead of making an unsupported assumption to complete the file.","caution":"Do not select fabric on price or hand feel alone before checking durability and behavior after laundering. Avoid turning a single test result into a conclusion about the entire system. A correct calculation can still lead to a wrong decision when its input data is outdated, and a passing specimen does not necessarily represent every lot unless variation has been assessed. When requirements, materials, suppliers, operating conditions or document revisions change, determine the scope of impact before proceeding.","deep":"Weight the material-selection matrix according to product function; do not automatically give the highest weight to price. For a running shirt, consider mass, moisture management, stretch and recovery, seam durability, perspiration colorfastness and comfort during movement. For a rain jacket, assess the membrane or coating construction, seam sealing, closure integrity, flex durability and water-vapor transmission. Compare samples under the same conditions, document trade-offs and confirm the choice through testing. A low purchase price may increase costs from defects, returns or warranty claims. Material selection should not begin with a fabric’s trade name. First describe the end-use environment, activity level, exposure to water or heat, laundering frequency, appearance requirements, expected service life and cost ceiling. Translate each requirement into an assessable property, select representative material samples and define suitable test methods. At every review, retain the reference sample, lot code, construction, composition, finish, test results and acceptance decision. If the supplier or construction changes, do not assume that previous results remain valid; reassess the properties that may be affected. For every result, record the test method, preconditioning conditions, sampling direction, number of specimens and units. Do not compare values obtained using different conditioning or test methods. With anisotropic materials, results in the machine and cross directions may differ substantially, so specimen orientation must remain consistent during both testing and interpretation. Laboratory results must be considered alongside the intended end use: a specimen that meets a mechanical requirement may still provide an unsuitable wearing experience or fail under actual laundering conditions. When a result is close to an acceptance limit, consider variation between specimens and lots rather than relying on the average alone. At a minimum, each practical assessment should retain source data, the decision made, the approver, supporting evidence and the next action if the result falls outside requirements. Effectiveness should be judged not merely by whether the task was completed, but by whether deviations were detected early, their causes could be traced and recurrence was prevented.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Selecting Materials for Their Intended Use\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Selecting Materials for Their Intended Use</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Selecting Materials for Their Intended Use</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Approval Workflow</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Function</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Criteria Matrix</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Testing</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Material Selection</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and related revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":4},"5":{"title":"Fabric Weight, Thickness and Density","cat":"Product Development and Material Selection","overview":"Fabric weight, thickness and density must be understood in relation to the complete product system. GSM is mass per unit area; thickness is the measured fabric depth under specified conditions; and yarn or loop density describes the compactness of the structure. These properties are related but are not interchangeable. In practice, learners must distinguish the input data, the decision to be made and the evidence required to validate that decision. Without all three, the work can remain at the level of a concept description rather than becoming a controlled process.","method":"Take representative specimens across the width and along the length of the roll. Condition them according to the applicable test method, use suitable instruments and record presser-foot pressure, measurement locations and repetitions. Compare results with the specification under equivalent conditions. Before finalizing a result, verify that the data is complete, the relevant documents are consistent and the approval authority is clear. Record who performed the work, when it was updated, which document revision was used and which assumptions remain valid. If information is missing or contradictory, record it as an item requiring confirmation instead of making an unsupported assumption to complete the file.","caution":"Thickness changes with measurement pressure and compressible structure. Do not treat results from different methods as directly equivalent. Avoid turning a single test result into a conclusion about the entire system. A correct calculation can still lead to a wrong decision when its input data is outdated, and a passing specimen does not necessarily represent every lot unless variation has been assessed. When requirements, materials, suppliers, operating conditions or document revisions change, determine the scope of impact before proceeding.","deep":"GSM is mass per unit area. Take representative specimens, condition them according to the applicable method, and use a cutter with a defined area and a suitable balance. Thickness depends on presser-foot pressure, dwell time and compressibility, so the method must be recorded. Warp and weft density, or wales and courses in knitted fabric, affect cover, drape and strength; identify the correct direction and measurement area. When variation occurs between the beginning and end of a roll, between rolls or between lots, map sampling locations rather than relying on a single measurement. For every result, record the test method, preconditioning conditions, sampling direction, number of specimens and units. Do not compare values obtained using different conditioning or test methods. With anisotropic materials, results in the machine and cross directions may differ substantially, so specimen orientation must remain consistent during both testing and interpretation. Laboratory results must be considered alongside the intended end use: a specimen that meets a mechanical requirement may still provide an unsuitable wearing experience or fail under actual laundering conditions. When a result is close to an acceptance limit, consider variation between specimens and lots rather than relying on the average alone. At a minimum, each practical assessment should retain source data, the decision made, the approver, supporting evidence and the next action if the result falls outside requirements. Effectiveness should be judged not merely by whether the task was completed, but by whether deviations were detected early, their causes could be traced and recurrence was prevented.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Fabric Weight, Thickness and Density\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Fabric Weight, Thickness and Density</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Fabric Weight, Thickness and Density</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Approval Workflow</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Sampling</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">GSM</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Thickness</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Density</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and related revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":5},"6":{"title":"Tensile Strength, Elongation and Tear Strength","cat":"Material Testing and Performance","overview":"Tensile strength, elongation and tear strength must be considered in relation to the complete product and operating system. Tensile strength is the force required to break a specimen under a defined test method; elongation describes deformation; and tear strength measures resistance to the propagation of a tear. Results depend on specimen direction and material construction. In practice, learners must distinguish the input data, the decision to be made and the evidence required to validate that decision. Without all three, the work can remain at the level of a concept description rather than becoming a controlled process.","method":"Select a method suitable for woven, knitted or coated materials. Take specimens in the warp and weft directions, or wale and course directions, as applicable. Record peak force, elongation, failure mode and preconditioning conditions. Analyze both the mean and the spread of results. Before finalizing a result, verify that the data is complete, the relevant documents are consistent and the approval authority is clear. Record who performed the work, when it was updated, which document revision was used and which assumptions remain valid. If information is missing or contradictory, record it as an item requiring confirmation instead of making an unsupported assumption to complete the file.","caution":"Do not use a single tensile result to conclude that the entire product is strong; seams and stress-concentration points may be weaker than the fabric. Avoid turning a single test result into a conclusion about the entire system. A correct calculation can still lead to a wrong decision when its input data is outdated, and a passing specimen does not necessarily represent every lot unless variation has been assessed. When requirements, materials, suppliers, operating conditions or document revisions change, determine the scope of impact before proceeding.","deep":"Distinguish breaking force, elongation at break, force at a specified extension and tear strength; they describe different failure mechanisms. Results depend on specimen direction and width, test speed, jaw separation, conditioning and method. Record machine and cross directions separately and identify failure modes such as yarn breakage, yarn slippage or jaw slippage. For finished products, also inspect seams, pocket positions, crotches, underarms and reinforcement points because garment geometry and stitch lines can concentrate stress. For every result, record the test method, preconditioning conditions, sampling direction, number of specimens and units. Do not compare values obtained using different conditioning or test methods. With anisotropic materials, results in the machine and cross directions may differ substantially, so specimen orientation must remain consistent during both testing and interpretation. Laboratory results must be considered alongside the intended end use: a specimen that meets a mechanical requirement may still provide an unsuitable wearing experience or fail under actual laundering conditions. When a result is close to an acceptance limit, consider variation between specimens and lots rather than relying on the average alone. At a minimum, each practical assessment should retain source data, the decision made, the approver, supporting evidence and the next action if the result falls outside requirements. Effectiveness should be judged not merely by whether the task was completed, but by whether deviations were detected early, their causes could be traced and recurrence was prevented.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Tensile Strength, Elongation and Tear Strength\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Tensile Strength, Elongation and Tear Strength</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Tensile Strength, Elongation and Tear Strength</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Approval Workflow</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Warp/Weft Specimens</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Tensile</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Elongation</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Tear</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and related revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":6},"7":{"title":"Abrasion Resistance and Pilling","cat":"Material Testing and Performance","overview":"Abrasion resistance and pilling must be considered in relation to the complete product and operating system. Abrasion is surface deterioration caused by repeated friction; pilling is the formation of small fiber balls on the surface. Fiber and yarn structure, fabric density, finishing and use conditions all influence performance. In practice, learners must distinguish the input data, the decision to be made and the evidence required to validate that decision. Without all three, the work can remain at the level of a concept description rather than becoming a controlled process.","method":"Select suitable equipment and a test method, such as Martindale or the method specified by the buyer. Record the number of cycles, pressure, abradant, rating scale and photographs of the surface before and after testing. Before finalizing a result, verify that the data is complete, the relevant documents are consistent and the approval authority is clear. Record who performed the work, when it was updated, which document revision was used and which assumptions remain valid. If information is missing or contradictory, record it as an item requiring confirmation instead of making an unsupported assumption to complete the file.","caution":"Results from different methods or rating scales must not be converted or compared casually. Avoid turning a single test result into a conclusion about the entire system. A correct calculation can still lead to a wrong decision when its input data is outdated, and a passing specimen does not necessarily represent every lot unless variation has been assessed. When requirements, materials, suppliers, operating conditions or document revisions change, determine the scope of impact before proceeding.","deep":"Abrasion testing evaluates resistance to rubbing under a defined apparatus and cycle; pilling assessment evaluates the formation of protruding fibers and pills on the surface. The two properties are related but are not interchangeable. Record the equipment, abradant, load, cycles, end-point criteria and visual rating method. For colored or patterned fabrics, viewing light and the photographic reference background can affect assessment. Examine both the tested fabric face and surface changes after laundering because finishing structures may change. For every result, record the test method, preconditioning conditions, sampling direction, number of specimens and units. Do not compare values obtained using different conditioning or test methods. With anisotropic materials, results in the machine and cross directions may differ substantially, so specimen orientation must remain consistent during both testing and interpretation. Laboratory results must be considered alongside the intended end use: a specimen that meets a mechanical requirement may still provide an unsuitable wearing experience or fail under actual laundering conditions. When a result is close to an acceptance limit, consider variation between specimens and lots rather than relying on the average alone. At a minimum, each practical assessment should retain source data, the decision made, the approver, supporting evidence and the next action if the result falls outside requirements. Effectiveness should be judged not merely by whether the task was completed, but by whether deviations were detected early, their causes could be traced and recurrence was prevented.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Abrasion Resistance and Pilling\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Abrasion Resistance and Pilling</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Abrasion Resistance and Pilling</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Approval Workflow</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Specimen Preparation</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Abrasion</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Pilling</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evaluation</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and related revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":7},"8":{"title":"Colorfastness and Color Staining","cat":"Material Testing and Performance","overview":"Colorfastness and color staining must be considered in relation to the complete product and operating system. Colorfastness describes a material’s ability to retain color when exposed to laundering, rubbing, perspiration, light or water. Staining of an adjacent material is a separate risk from color change in the test specimen. In practice, learners must distinguish the input data, the decision to be made and the evidence required to validate that decision. Without all three, the work can remain at the level of a concept description rather than becoming a controlled process.","method":"Identify the intended use environment and select the relevant tests. Record the gray scale, test conditions, adjacent fabric type, rubbing direction, laundering cycles and buyer criteria. Evaluate both color change and staining. Before finalizing a result, verify that the data is complete, the relevant documents are consistent and the approval authority is clear. Record who performed the work, when it was updated, which document revision was used and which assumptions remain valid. If information is missing or contradictory, record it as an item requiring confirmation instead of making an unsupported assumption to complete the file.","caution":"Passing one colorfastness test does not prove that a material will perform under every condition of use. Avoid turning a single test result into a conclusion about the entire system. A correct calculation can still lead to a wrong decision when its input data is outdated, and a passing specimen does not necessarily represent every lot unless variation has been assessed. When requirements, materials, suppliers, operating conditions or document revisions change, determine the scope of impact before proceeding.","deep":"Assess colorfastness separately for laundering, dry and wet rubbing, perspiration, water, light and color transfer on contact. Each test has its own conditions, adjacent material and evaluation method. Distinguish a color change in the specimen from staining transferred to the adjacent fabric; the two results may differ. When investigating a complaint, retain the original specimen, tested specimen, color code, dye lot, finishing recipe and laundering conditions. Do not combine all results under a generic “colorfastness” label without identifying the specific tests. Performance must be evaluated under conditions representative of product use and care. A material may meet its initial specification but change after washing, drying, abrasion or perspiration exposure. Specify whether testing is performed before or after treatment, the number of cycles, detergent type and drying conditions required by the method. When a result fails, separate the observed effect from the suspected cause. Color change, bleeding, shrinkage or reduced moisture transmission may relate to fiber, dye, construction, finishing or care procedures. Confirm the cause only when a test or control can distinguish between the possible explanations. For every result, record the test method, preconditioning conditions, sampling direction, number of specimens and units. Do not compare values obtained using different conditioning or test methods. With anisotropic materials, results in the machine and cross directions may differ substantially, so specimen orientation must remain consistent during both testing and interpretation. Laboratory results must be considered alongside the intended end use: a specimen that meets a mechanical requirement may still provide an unsuitable wearing experience or fail under actual laundering conditions. When a result is close to an acceptance limit, consider variation between specimens and lots rather than relying on the average alone. At a minimum, each practical assessment should retain source data, the decision made, the approver, supporting evidence and the next action if the result falls outside requirements. Effectiveness should be judged not merely by whether the task was completed, but by whether deviations were detected early, their causes could be traced and recurrence was prevented.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Colorfastness and Color Staining\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Colorfastness and Color Staining</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Colorfastness and Color Staining</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Approval Workflow</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Exposure</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Color Change Rating</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Staining Rating</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and related revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":8},"9":{"title":"Shrinkage, Growth and Dimensional Stability","cat":"Material Testing and Performance","overview":"Shrinkage, growth and dimensional stability must be considered in relation to the complete product and operating system. Fabric dimensions may change after washing, drying, steaming, pressing or relaxation. Knitted fabrics often behave differently from woven fabrics, and lengthwise and widthwise changes may differ. In practice, learners must distinguish the input data, the decision to be made and the evidence required to validate that decision. Without all three, the work can remain at the level of a concept description rather than becoming a controlled process.","method":"Mark the specimen by direction, measure its initial dimensions, apply the specified process, recondition it and measure again. Calculate percentage change separately in length and width. Test multiple cycles when required by the applicable standard. Before finalizing a result, verify that the data is complete, the relevant documents are consistent and the approval authority is clear. Record who performed the work, when it was updated, which document revision was used and which assumptions remain valid. If information is missing or contradictory, record it as an item requiring confirmation instead of making an unsupported assumption to complete the file.","caution":"Do not apply one general shrinkage allowance to every fiber composition, construction and care regime. Avoid turning a single test result into a conclusion about the entire system. A correct calculation can still lead to a wrong decision when its input data is outdated, and a passing specimen does not necessarily represent every lot unless variation has been assessed. When requirements, materials, suppliers, operating conditions or document revisions change, determine the scope of impact before proceeding.","deep":"Shrinkage is a dimensional change after processing; growth is an increase in dimensions under use or load. Mark gauge distances in the length and width directions before treatment. Record the washing and drying procedure, number of cycles and recovery time before remeasurement. Separate fabric dimensional change from seam distortion or deformation of the finished garment. For stretch fabrics, assess growth under load and recovery after rest. Results must be linked to the intended care regime and material revision. Performance must be evaluated under conditions representative of product use and care. A material may meet its initial specification but change after washing, drying, abrasion or perspiration exposure. Specify whether testing is performed before or after treatment, the number of cycles, detergent type and drying conditions required by the method. When a result fails, separate the observed effect from the suspected cause. Color change, bleeding, shrinkage or reduced moisture transmission may relate to fiber, dye, construction, finishing or care procedures. Confirm the cause only when a test or control can distinguish between the possible explanations. For every result, record the test method, preconditioning conditions, sampling direction, number of specimens and units. Do not compare values obtained using different conditioning or test methods. With anisotropic materials, results in the machine and cross directions may differ substantially, so specimen orientation must remain consistent during both testing and interpretation. Laboratory results must be considered alongside the intended end use: a specimen that meets a mechanical requirement may still provide an unsuitable wearing experience or fail under actual laundering conditions. When a result is close to an acceptance limit, consider variation between specimens and lots rather than relying on the average alone. At a minimum, each practical assessment should retain source data, the decision made, the approver, supporting evidence and the next action if the result falls outside requirements. Effectiveness should be judged not merely by whether the task was completed, but by whether deviations were detected early, their causes could be traced and recurrence was prevented.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Shrinkage, Growth and Dimensional Stability\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Shrinkage, Growth and Dimensional Stability</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Shrinkage, Growth and Dimensional Stability</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Approval Workflow</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Marking</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Washing/Treatment</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Relaxation</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Remeasurement</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and related revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":9},"10":{"title":"Air Permeability and Moisture Vapor Transmission","cat":"Material Testing and Performance","overview":"Air permeability and moisture vapor transmission must be considered in relation to the complete product and operating system. Air permeability measures airflow through a material under a specified pressure difference. Water-vapor transmission measures the passage of water vapor; the two tests represent different mechanisms. In practice, learners must distinguish the input data, the decision to be made and the evidence required to validate that decision. Without all three, the work can remain at the level of a concept description rather than becoming a controlled process.","method":"Select the test according to the intended performance: breathability, wind resistance, vapor release or waterproofing. Record pressure difference, test area, temperature and humidity, and specimen construction. Test multilayer assemblies when the product uses a laminate. Before finalizing a result, verify that the data is complete, the relevant documents are consistent and the approval authority is clear. Record who performed the work, when it was updated, which document revision was used and which assumptions remain valid. If information is missing or contradictory, record it as an item requiring confirmation instead of making an unsupported assumption to complete the file.","caution":"Do not equate air permeability with moisture absorption or quick drying. Avoid turning a single test result into a conclusion about the entire system. A correct calculation can still lead to a wrong decision when its input data is outdated, and a passing specimen does not necessarily represent every lot unless variation has been assessed. When requirements, materials, suppliers, operating conditions or document revisions change, determine the scope of impact before proceeding.","deep":"Air permeability measures the volume of air passing through a material area under a specified pressure differential. Moisture-vapor transmission can be evaluated by different methods and is not the same as airflow. A coated membrane may reduce air permeability while still allowing water vapor to pass, depending on its construction. When comparing results, record test area, pressure differential, conditioning, test face and method. For multilayer products, evaluate the combined assembly because lining, seams and coatings can change performance compared with the base fabric. Performance must be evaluated under conditions representative of product use and care. A material may meet its initial specification but change after washing, drying, abrasion or perspiration exposure. Specify whether testing is performed before or after treatment, the number of cycles, detergent type and drying conditions required by the method. When a result fails, separate the observed effect from the suspected cause. Color change, bleeding, shrinkage or reduced moisture transmission may relate to fiber, dye, construction, finishing or care procedures. Confirm the cause only when a test or control can distinguish between the possible explanations. For every result, record the test method, preconditioning conditions, sampling direction, number of specimens and units. Do not compare values obtained using different conditioning or test methods. With anisotropic materials, results in the machine and cross directions may differ substantially, so specimen orientation must remain consistent during both testing and interpretation. Laboratory results must be considered alongside the intended end use: a specimen that meets a mechanical requirement may still provide an unsuitable wearing experience or fail under actual laundering conditions. When a result is close to an acceptance limit, consider variation between specimens and lots rather than relying on the average alone. At a minimum, each practical assessment should retain source data, the decision made, the approver, supporting evidence and the next action if the result falls outside requirements. Effectiveness should be judged not merely by whether the task was completed, but by whether deviations were detected early, their causes could be traced and recurrence was prevented.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Air Permeability and Moisture Vapor Transmission\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Air Permeability and Moisture Vapor Transmission</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Air Permeability and Moisture Vapor Transmission</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Approval Workflow</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Pressure Differential</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Airflow</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Vapor Transmission</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Layer Comparison</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and related revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":10},"11":{"title":"Moisture Absorption, Wicking and Quick Drying","overview":"Moisture absorption, wicking and drying rate must be considered as distinct properties within the complete product system. Absorbency describes a material’s capacity to take up liquid; wicking is the movement of liquid through capillary pathways; drying rate depends on evaporation, construction and environmental conditions. A fabric may absorb little liquid yet spread moisture rapidly, or absorb a great deal and retain it. Learners must identify the input data, the decision to be made and the evidence needed to validate that decision. Without these elements, the subject remains descriptive rather than a controlled assessment process.","method":"Start with the end-use requirement and source data before selecting a test or record form. Define the intended application and select relevant measurements, such as wetting time, capillary rise, spreading area and residual moisture over time. Keep temperature, relative humidity and airflow consistent. Before approving the result, check data completeness, consistency between records and the authority responsible for approval. Record the operator, update date, document revision and assumptions that remain valid. Treat missing or conflicting information as an open verification item; do not fill gaps by guesswork.","caution":"Do not use hand feel as a substitute for a controlled moisture-management measurement. A single result must not be generalized to the whole product system. A correct calculation can still produce a wrong decision if its inputs are obsolete, and one passing specimen may not represent every lot when variation has not been assessed. Reassess the impact whenever requirements, materials, suppliers, operating conditions or document revisions change.","deep":"Absorption describes how much moisture a material takes up; wicking describes liquid movement along its structure; drying rate is affected by initial moisture content, temperature, humidity, airflow and construction. A highly absorbent fabric may retain water for a long time, while a low-absorbency fabric may spread perspiration across its surface. Define the end-use objective first, then select a suitable measurement: wetting time, capillary height, spreading rate or mass loss over time. Record the tested face, specimen direction and finishing condition. Assess performance under conditions representative of product use and care. A material may perform well when new but change after laundering, drying, abrasion or perspiration exposure. Specify whether testing is conducted before or after treatment, the number of cycles, detergent type and drying conditions required by the selected method. When a result fails, separate the observed failure from the proposed cause. Discoloration, staining, shrinkage or reduced moisture transport may relate to fibre, dye, construction, finish or care process. Confirm the cause only with a test or control capable of distinguishing these possibilities. A practical record should retain sourced input data, the decision, approver, supporting evidence and follow-up action for an out-of-specification result. Evaluate whether deviations were detected early, traced to their causes and prevented from recurring, not merely whether the task was completed.","cat":"Material Testing and Performance","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Moisture absorption, wicking and quick drying\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Moisture absorption, wicking and quick drying</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Moisture absorption, wicking and quick drying</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material assessment and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Absorption</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Moisture spreading</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evaporation</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Quick drying</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"12":{"title":"Water Repellency and Hydrostatic Resistance","overview":"Water repellency and hydrostatic resistance are different performance properties and must be evaluated separately. Water repellency generally describes the tendency of droplets not to wet a surface; hydrostatic head measures the water pressure a material withstands before penetration occurs. Both must be interpreted in relation to the complete product and its intended use. Learners should distinguish source data, the decision required and the evidence used to validate it. Without these elements, assessment can remain a description rather than a controlled process.","method":"Begin with the product requirement and source data. Establish whether the product is intended for light rain, prolonged rain or exposure to sustained water pressure. Inspect the outer face, seams, zippers and construction junctions, including after laundering. Record the test method, pressure, exposure time and leakage location. Verify data completeness, consistency across documents and approval authority. Record the operator, date, document revision and current assumptions. Escalate missing or conflicting information for confirmation rather than making unsupported assumptions.","caution":"A fabric passing a hydrostatic test does not prove that a finished jacket is waterproof if its seams or closure system fail. Do not turn an isolated test result into a conclusion about the complete product. Outdated input data or unassessed lot variation can invalidate an otherwise correct interpretation. Reassess the impact of changes to requirements, materials, suppliers, operating conditions and document revisions.","deep":"Surface water repellency concerns whether droplets wet the fabric face; hydrostatic head measures resistance to water pressure before penetration. These are not interchangeable properties. On a finished garment, seams, zippers, plackets, pockets and junctions can become leakage paths. Record the specimen construction, tested face, preconditioning and exact leakage location. Where seam tape is used, assess adhesion and continuity, especially at seam intersections. Evaluate performance under representative use and care conditions. A material may pass when new but deteriorate after washing, drying, abrasion or perspiration exposure. Define pre- or post-treatment testing, cycle count, detergent and drying conditions according to the selected method. For failures, distinguish observed evidence from a suspected cause. Discoloration, staining, shrinkage or reduced moisture transport may arise from fibres, dyes, construction, finishes or care processes; confirm the cause with discriminating tests or controls. Retain traceable input data, the decision, approver, evidence and corrective action. Review whether deviations were detected early, their causes traced and recurrence prevented.","cat":"Material Testing and Performance","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Water repellency and hydrostatic resistance\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Water repellency and hydrostatic resistance</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Water repellency and hydrostatic resistance</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material assessment and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Spray test</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Water pressure</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Seams</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Finished garment</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"13":{"title":"Coated Fabrics and Laminates","overview":"Coated fabrics and laminates use different layer-building approaches. Coating forms a polymer layer on a textile substrate, while lamination bonds a film or membrane to a substrate, commonly with adhesive or heat. Multilayer structures can add functionality but may change drape, breathability, repairability and processing behaviour. Assessment must connect material structure to the complete product and operating system. Learners should identify input data, decisions and validation evidence before selecting a process.","method":"Review the membrane or coating type, adhesive layer, textile substrate, bond strength, flex durability, hydrolysis resistance, delamination and post-laundering performance. Inspect cross-sections, cut edges, seams and fold zones. Confirm that the data is complete, documents agree and approval authority is defined. Record the operator, update date, revision and assumptions. Missing or contradictory information must remain an explicit verification item.","caution":"Do not limit inspection to a new, flat specimen. Delamination can emerge after repeated flexing, heat, moisture exposure or ageing. A single passing result does not establish performance across the complete product or every production lot. Reassess when material specifications, suppliers, processing conditions or document revisions change.","deep":"A coated fabric carries a polymer layer formed on a textile substrate; a laminate bonds a membrane or film by adhesive, heat or another controlled process. Evaluate adhesion, coating uniformity, cracking, hydrolysis, delamination and changes after flexing or laundering. Identify the face, back, substrate, coating or membrane and adhesive layer where present. Defects may concentrate at cut edges, folds, heat-pressed areas or zones under tension. During sewing, validate needle, thread, presser-foot and pressing settings on the actual construction to avoid puncturing the membrane or damaging the coating. For multilayer or stretch materials, assess both initial condition and performance after repeated exposure. Check interlayer bonding, cracking, delamination and drape changes after flexing or washing. For stretch materials, record applied load, extension, hold time and recovery time; without these conditions, results are difficult to compare. Test representative sewn assemblies because seams, bonded edges, linings and stress points can alter performance. A flat material specimen provides only part of the evidence and cannot replace testing of a representative product or component.","cat":"Material Testing and Performance","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Coated fabrics and laminates\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Coated fabrics and laminates</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Coated fabrics and laminates</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material assessment and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Textile substrate</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Coating or membrane</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Layer bonding</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Adhesion</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"14":{"title":"Stretch and Recovery Materials","overview":"Stretch and recovery describe related but separate aspects of material behaviour. Stretch is the extension produced under a defined load; recovery is the ability to return towards the original dimensions after the load is removed. Power and growth influence wearing comfort, fit retention and shape stability. These properties must be evaluated against the garment’s intended use and construction. Learners must connect input data, decisions and validation evidence rather than relying on a general description of elasticity.","method":"Define the required extension, load, direction, hold duration and recovery period from the product specification. Test in the relevant fabric directions and record specimen dimensions, conditioning, applied force and residual growth. Assess the material in the intended garment construction and after relevant care cycles. Check that test data, document revisions and approval authority are consistent; record the operator, date and assumptions. Escalate missing or conflicting evidence for confirmation.","caution":"A high stretch percentage does not automatically mean good recovery or a stable garment fit. Results cannot be compared reliably when load, extension, hold time, specimen direction or recovery interval differ. Assess lot variation and the effect of laundering, heat and repeated wear before approving a material.","deep":"Stretch is the extension of a material under a specified force; recovery is the dimensional return after that force is released. Growth is the residual extension remaining after loading, while power describes the force associated with stretching and the resulting resistance felt by the wearer. These measures influence fit, comfort and garment shape retention. Record the direction of test, load, extension, time under load and recovery interval. Knit and woven constructions, elastane content, yarn arrangement and finishing can produce different behaviour. Evaluate both lengthwise and crosswise directions where relevant. Test after repeated extension and after care treatments representative of use. At product level, consider pattern ease, seam extensibility, stitch type, thread behaviour and panel orientation. A fabric that performs well in a strip test may still cause seam grin, bagging at knees or elbows, or restricted movement in a finished garment. Use a representative sewn sample and retain the material lot, test conditions, results and approval decision.","cat":"Material Testing and Performance","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Stretch and recovery materials\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Stretch and recovery materials</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Stretch and recovery materials</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material assessment and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Applied load</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Extension</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Load removal</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Residual growth</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"15":{"title":"Elastane and Stretch-Yarn Structures","overview":"Elastane and stretch-yarn structures must be assessed as part of the complete material and manufacturing system. Elastane may be covered, core-spun or otherwise combined with base fibres in yarn and fabric structures. The elastane percentage alone does not determine stretch performance; construction, feed tension and finishing are also important. Learners must identify the source data, decision and validation evidence required for a controlled material approval.","method":"Verify fibre composition, yarn structure, elastane feed percentage, recovery, processing temperature and compatibility with dyeing and finishing. Control actual material temperature during drying, pressing and heat setting against validated supplier guidance. Before approval, check data completeness, document consistency and approval authority. Record operator, update date, revision and assumptions; escalate missing or conflicting information.","caution":"Do not apply one processing temperature to every elastane type or blended fabric. Excess heat, dwell time or tension can reduce elasticity and yarn strength. A single passing test does not establish consistency across production lots; reassess when material, supplier or process conditions change.","deep":"Elastane may be covered or combined with a base yarn in different structures; percentage content alone is insufficient to describe performance. Dyeing, heat setting, drying and pressing can alter elasticity and yarn strength. Control the actual material temperature, dwell time, tension and machine speed within a validated process window. Avoid excessive extension during spreading, sewing and finishing. Compare stretch and recovery before and after processing to identify deterioration. For multilayer or stretch materials, assess both initial condition and performance after repeated exposure. With laminates, inspect interlayer bonding, delamination, cracking and drape changes after flexing or washing. For stretch materials, record test load, extension, hold time and recovery time so results can be compared. At product level, test the complete sewn construction because seams, bonded areas, linings and stress points can change performance. A flat specimen provides only part of the evidence and cannot replace representative product or component testing. Retain source data, decision, approver, evidence and follow-up action for nonconforming results.","cat":"Material Testing and Performance","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Elastane and Stretch-Yarn Structures\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Elastane and Stretch-Yarn Structures</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Elastane and Stretch-Yarn Structures</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material assessment and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Yarn construction</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Heat treatment</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Tension</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Recovery</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"16":{"title":"Functional Fabrics for Sportswear and Outdoor Use","overview":"Functional fabrics for sportswear and outdoor use may combine moisture management, wind resistance, rain protection, UV protection, odour control, abrasion resistance and thermal insulation. Each functional claim requires evidence from an appropriate test. The relevant properties depend on activity, body zone, exposure and garment construction. Learners must distinguish input data, decisions and validation evidence so that material selection is controlled and claims remain within their demonstrated scope.","method":"Build a claim matrix linking each marketing statement to the material mechanism, test method, test conditions, use limitations, durability after laundering and supporting records. Consider skin contact, environmental exposure and care instructions. Confirm that the evidence is complete and consistent, and record the approver, revision and assumptions. Treat gaps or contradictions as items requiring confirmation.","caution":"Do not use claims such as antibacterial, UV-protective or waterproof without valid test results and a clearly defined evidence scope. A material may perform differently after washing, abrasion, sweat exposure or use in a finished garment. Reassess any change in requirement, material, supplier or process.","deep":"Select fabric according to the activity. Running apparel needs effective sweat management and low-friction comfort; trekking garments need durability, quick drying and effective layering; rainwear requires water resistance in both the material and finished construction. Assess performance by body zone and stress point rather than relying on a single flat specimen. For outdoor use, also consider UV exposure, backpack abrasion, perspiration, mud and care conditions. Each claim must be linked to a test method, specimen configuration and use limitation. Test a representative finished sample in an activity that reflects intended use before approving the material. Material evaluation must connect end use, product construction and defined test conditions. No single indicator establishes overall quality or suitability. Retain sample ID, material lot, test method and conditions. Supplier and material-version comparisons require equivalent sampling and testing. The assessment file should identify sourced data, the decision, approver, supporting evidence and follow-up action where results fall outside requirements.","cat":"Functional and Sustainable Materials","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Functional Fabrics for Sportswear and Outdoor Use\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Functional Fabrics for Sportswear and Outdoor Use</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Functional Fabrics for Sportswear and Outdoor Use</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material assessment and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Activity</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Environment</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Performance</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Wear trial</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"17":{"title":"Flame-Retardant and Protective Materials","overview":"Protective clothing must be designed around a specific hazard, which may include heat, flame, electric arc, chemicals, electrostatic discharge or mechanical exposure. The textile is only one element of the protective system. Material selection must therefore begin with a documented hazard assessment, the required protection level and the applicable product standard. Learners must distinguish source data, decisions and validation evidence before approving a protective material or garment.","method":"Identify the hazard assessment, product standard and required protection class. Review seams, accessories, closures, coverage and maintenance requirements, and test the finished construction where the standard requires it. Verify data completeness, document consistency and approval authority. Record the operator, update date, revision and assumptions. Escalate missing or conflicting information instead of making unsupported conclusions.","caution":"Do not claim that PPE complies with a standard based only on a certificate for one fabric or component. A material-level result does not prove whole-garment protection. Changes in supplier, composition, finish, construction or care instructions may affect the validity of test evidence and require reassessment.","deep":"Flame-retardant materials may be designed to limit ignition, flame spread, molten dripping or heat transfer; these are different performance characteristics. Identify the actual hazard and the standard required for the finished product before selecting fabric. Multilayer construction, seams, closures, accessories and laundering all affect performance. A certificate for the base fabric does not automatically apply to the complete garment. Track supplier changes, finishing formulations and care instructions because they may alter performance. Select protective materials against the identified hazard and the standard applicable to the market, industry or workplace. Do not extend one test result into a claim of total protection. Review multilayer construction, seams, accessories, coverage, laundering durability and actual wearing conditions. The technical file should state the standard and edition, test configuration, results, use limitations and care instructions. If supplier, composition, dye, coating or sewing construction changes, determine whether existing evidence remains valid. Retain sourced input data, decision, approver, supporting records and follow-up action for any result outside requirements.","cat":"Functional and Sustainable Materials","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Flame-Retardant and Protective Materials\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Flame-Retardant and Protective Materials</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Flame-Retardant and Protective Materials</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material assessment and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Hazard</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Standard</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Garment configuration</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Validation</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"18":{"title":"Antistatic and Conductive Materials","overview":"Antistatic and conductive materials are used to manage electrostatic charge, which can cause discomfort, attract dust or create an ignition risk in flammable environments. Performance depends on the conductive pathway, humidity, material construction and grounding arrangements in the actual use system. A conductive fibre alone does not establish safe operation. Learners must connect the identified workplace hazard to the required material properties, system controls and validation evidence.","method":"Define the workplace environment and applicable electrostatic-control requirements. Measure surface or volume resistance, or charge decay, using the relevant standard and controlled conditioning. Record relative humidity because it can influence results. Inspect continuity across seams and contact zones, then assess performance after laundering, abrasion and ageing. Consider the complete clothing and grounding system. Record test method, specimen configuration, results, limitations and approval.","caution":"A fabric containing conductive fibres does not automatically make a garment safe in a hazardous area. Clothing is only one element of electrostatic control; footwear, flooring, grounding and operating procedures may also be necessary. Do not make a broad safety claim from an isolated fabric measurement.","deep":"Antistatic materials may dissipate charge through conductive fibres, surface structures or a grounded system. Measure surface or volume resistance, or charge-decay time, using a suitable method and controlled humidity. Check electrical continuity at seams and body-contact areas. In explosive atmospheres or around electrostatic-sensitive equipment, garments form only one part of the control system; footwear, floor properties, grounding and work procedures must be considered together. Select protective materials against the identified hazard and the standard applicable to the market, industry or workplace. Do not generalize one test result into a claim of complete protection. Consider multilayer construction, seams, accessories, coverage, laundering durability and actual wearing practice. The technical file should identify the standard and edition, specimen configuration, results, use limitations and care instructions. Changes to supplier, composition, dye, coating or sewing construction may invalidate previous evidence and must be reviewed.","cat":"Functional and Sustainable Materials","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Antistatic and conductive materials\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Antistatic and conductive materials</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Antistatic and conductive materials</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material assessment and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Charge</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">generation</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Dissipation</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Resistance measurement</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">System</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"19":{"title":"Recycled Materials and Traceability","overview":"Recycled fibres may originate from pre-consumer or post-consumer waste streams, which can differ in composition, consistency and contamination risk. A recycled-content claim requires evidence across the relevant supply chain and must remain within the scope of the applicable certification or verification. Learners must distinguish material origin, mass-based content, traceability records and the exact product or lot covered by the claim.","method":"Request supplier declarations and, where required by the certification programme, transaction certificates and chain-of-custody records. Verify recycled content by mass and identify the certified product, facility and shipment scope. Check consistency between invoices, lot records and supporting certificates. Use composition testing where appropriate, recognizing its limits. Record document validity, scope, revision, responsible reviewer and unresolved discrepancies.","caution":"Do not infer that an entire garment is recycled because one input material carries a recycled-content certificate. Confirm the percentage calculation, denominator, supply-chain scope and covered product. A certificate outside its validity period or product scope cannot support the claim.","deep":"Traceability links a claim to records from input material and supplier through production lots to the finished product. Distinguish the recycled percentage by mass, the source of the recycled feedstock and the product scope supported by evidence. Check certificate validity, expiry, scope and chain-of-custody requirements; reconcile invoices, transaction certificates and equivalent records under the applicable programme. Where recycled and virgin inputs are blended, calculate the percentage on a mass basis and state the denominator. Do not extend a component-level claim to the whole garment without evidence. Material performance must still be assessed against intended use, construction and defined test conditions. Retain sample identification, lot, method and conditions. When comparing suppliers or material versions, ensure samples are taken and tested comparably. The file should retain sourced input data, the decision, approver, evidence and follow-up action for any nonconformity.","cat":"Functional and Sustainable Materials","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Recycled materials and traceability\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Recycled materials and traceability</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Recycled materials and traceability</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material assessment and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Fibre source</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Supplier</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Lot documentation</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Product claim</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"20":{"title":"Organic Cotton, Man-Made Cellulosics and Bio-Based Materials","overview":"Organic cotton, man-made cellulosic fibres and bio-based materials differ in feedstock, manufacturing route and environmental impacts. Organic cotton, viscose, modal, lyocell and emerging bio-based materials cannot be judged sustainable from fibre names alone. Assessment requires evidence about agricultural or forestry inputs, chemical management, water, energy, emissions and the scope of relevant certifications. Learners must connect material choice to equivalent product function and a clearly defined comparison boundary.","method":"Review feedstock origin, chemical inputs, water and energy use, emissions, land or forest management and relevant certification systems. Compare materials using equivalent product functions, system boundaries and functional units. Verify source documentation, production route and the scope of each claim. Check that the underlying data is complete, current and consistent, and record assumptions and approval. Avoid unsupported environmental conclusions where evidence is incomplete.","caution":"Avoid broad or vague green claims. State the scope, method and evidence supporting each environmental statement. Bio-based does not mean biodegradable in every environment, and a fibre certificate does not establish that the complete garment is sustainable.","deep":"Organic cotton, man-made cellulosics and bio-based materials have different feedstocks, processing routes and impact profiles. For regenerated cellulose, distinguish the source of dissolving pulp, the dissolution and regeneration process, and the chemical-recovery and control system. Bio-based origin does not mean that a material will biodegrade in every environment; biodegradation depends on conditions, construction and the test method. Verify feedstock records, manufacturing process and the exact scope of each claim. Environmental comparisons should use the same product function, system boundary and functional unit. Material suitability must also be linked to end use, garment construction and defined test conditions. No single indicator can establish overall quality or sustainability. Retain sample and lot identification, test method and conditions, and ensure supplier comparisons use equivalent sampling and testing. The assessment record should identify sourced data, the decision, approver, supporting evidence and follow-up action where requirements are not met.","cat":"Functional and Sustainable Materials","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Organic cotton, man-made cellulosics and bio-based materials\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Organic cotton, man-made cellulosics and bio-based materials</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Organic cotton, man-made cellulosics and bio-based materials</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material assessment and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Feedstock</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">source</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Process</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Certification</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Claim scope</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"21":{"title":"Restricted Chemicals and Material Compliance Management","overview":"Chemical compliance begins with identifying the substances that may be present in fibres, dyes, auxiliaries, coatings and finishing agents. Requirements can come from legislation, restricted-substance lists (RSLs), manufacturing restricted-substance lists (MRSLs), customer standards and product-specific rules. A material cannot be declared compliant solely because a supplier provides a general certificate; the evidence must match the material, process, facility, test method and applicable requirement. Learners must distinguish substances intentionally used in manufacturing from residues that may remain in the finished article.","method":"Map chemical inputs across the material supply chain, obtain current supplier declarations and safety or technical documentation, and identify applicable RSL/MRSL limits. Define risk-based testing by material type, colour, finish, process and supplier history. Confirm laboratory competence, test method, reporting limit and sample traceability. Record the requirement version, result, non-conformity, corrective action and approval status before releasing the material.","caution":"Do not treat an undated declaration, generic certificate or test report for a different colour, finish, mill or material construction as proof of compliance. A result below a reporting limit is not the same as proof that a substance is completely absent.","deep":"Chemical compliance must be managed from input selection through manufacturing and finished-product verification. Identify relevant chemical groups in dyes, pigments, auxiliaries, resins, water-repellent treatments, prints, adhesives and coatings. An RSL normally addresses substances restricted in the finished product, whereas an MRSL addresses chemicals restricted from intentional use in manufacturing; the exact scope depends on the programme or customer requirement. Build a material risk profile using fibre composition, colour depth, finishing route, process location, supplier controls and prior results. Specify the applicable list and revision, limits, test method, sample preparation, reporting limit and decision rule. Supplier declarations are useful evidence but should be supported by process controls and risk-based laboratory testing. When a result fails, contain the affected lot, trace products and orders, investigate the chemical source, agree corrective action and retest representative material. Retain formulation or input records where available, supplier declarations, test reports, lot references, corrective actions and the final release decision. Do not extend a pass result to untested materials or processes without a documented equivalence assessment.","cat":"Functional and Sustainable Materials","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Restricted Chemicals and Material Compliance Management\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Restricted Chemicals and Material Compliance Management</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Restricted Chemicals and Material Compliance Management</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material analysis and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">RSL/MRSL</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Market</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Sampling</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Corrective action</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"22":{"title":"Design for Manufacturability and Assembly","overview":"Design for manufacturability and assembly considers whether a selected material can be cut, handled, sewn, joined, finished and inspected consistently at the intended production scale. Fabric behaviour, thickness, stretch, fraying, surface friction, layer build-up and dimensional stability influence operation settings and construction choices. A design that performs well as a sample may create feeding, seam, pressing or assembly problems on the production line. Material and construction decisions therefore need to be evaluated together.","method":"Review the garment construction and operation sequence with material specifications and representative samples. Identify critical operations such as spreading, cutting, edge handling, sewing, bonding, pressing and finishing. Run a trial on representative equipment and record machine settings, needle and thread selection, seam performance, handling issues, rework and output stability. Revise the construction or process only after confirming that product performance and appearance remain acceptable.","caution":"Do not assume that a material is production-ready because a prototype can be assembled by an experienced sample machinist. Trial conditions must represent the equipment, operator skill and production method expected in bulk.","deep":"Manufacturability is the interaction between material behaviour, pattern geometry, garment construction, equipment and operator method. A high-stretch fabric may require controlled relaxation, spreading tension and cutting conditions; a slippery lining can shift between plies; a coated or laminated material may respond poorly to needle penetration, heat or adhesive bonding. Thick intersections can create excessive seam bulk, while loosely constructed or fraying fabrics may require edge stabilisation and adjusted handling. Map the material-related risks to each operation, including spreading, cutting, bundling, sewing, seam sealing, pressing, washing and final inspection. Evaluate seam type, stitch density, needle point, thread, presser-foot pressure, feed system, seam allowance and reinforcement using the actual material combination. A pilot should capture defects, cycle time, operator handling, rework and appearance after relevant finishing or laundering. Where a change improves assembly but affects drape, comfort, strength or appearance, assess the trade-off against the product specification. Record the approved construction, process window, equipment assumptions, trial results and unresolved limitations before bulk release.","cat":"Functional and Sustainable Materials","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Design for Manufacturability and Assembly\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Design for Manufacturability and Assembly</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Design for Manufacturability and Assembly</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material analysis and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Design</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">DFM review</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Sewing trial</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Validation</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"23":{"title":"Material and Trims Compatibility","overview":"A garment is a system of interacting materials: shell fabric, lining, interlining, sewing thread, zippers, buttons, elastics, labels, prints, adhesives and other trims. Components that pass their own individual tests may still behave differently when combined. Differences in shrinkage, heat response, colourfastness, chemical composition, stiffness or surface properties can cause puckering, staining, delamination, distortion or premature failure. Compatibility must therefore be assessed at both component and assembled-product level.","method":"Create a component matrix linking each material and trim to its contact partners, process exposures and performance requirements. Check dimensional change, colour transfer, heat and pressure tolerance, chemical interaction, corrosion or staining risk, adhesion and care-process compatibility as relevant. Build representative assemblies and expose them to the intended sewing, pressing, washing or use conditions. Record results by component lot and approve the complete combination, not just the individual parts.","caution":"Do not infer compatibility from separate supplier certificates or from a visual check on unused components. The assembled combination and its actual processing conditions must be evaluated.","deep":"Compatibility problems often emerge at interfaces. A fusible interlining can shrink differently from the shell and cause bubbling; a dark trim may transfer colour onto a light fabric; a metal component may corrode under perspiration or laundering; an adhesive may lose bond strength under heat, moisture or repeated flexing. Map direct contact, adjacent contact and process exposure for each component. Consider fibre and polymer chemistry, dye systems, coatings, finishes, pH, heat, pressure, moisture, friction and care instructions. Select tests according to the failure mechanism: dimensional stability for differential shrinkage, colourfastness or migration tests for staining, bond or peel tests for adhesion, corrosion assessment for metal trims, and repeated-use or laundering trials for durability. Test representative production combinations, including colourways and material lots with the highest plausible risk. If a supplier, finish, colour or construction changes, reassess the affected interfaces. Record component identification, assembly method, process settings, test conditions, failures, corrective action and the approved combination. A component-level pass must not be treated as proof that the complete garment system is compatible.","cat":"Design, Compatibility and Validation","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Material and Trims Compatibility\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Material and Trims Compatibility</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Material and Trims Compatibility</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material analysis and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Shell fabric</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Trims</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assembly</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Compatibility test</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"24":{"title":"Wear Trials and User Evaluation","overview":"Wear trials assess how a garment performs on people under defined use conditions. They can reveal comfort, mobility, thermal sensation, fit stability, pressure points, noise, skin irritation, fastening problems and failures that laboratory tests may not capture. Results depend on wearer characteristics, garment size, activity, climate, duration and evaluation method. A useful trial therefore requires a clear objective, representative participants and a structured record rather than informal comments alone.","method":"Define the intended user, size range, activities, environment, trial duration and questions before recruiting participants. Use garments made from identified production-representative materials and record fit, movement, comfort, visible changes and failures at planned intervals. Combine structured ratings with open comments and, where relevant, objective measurements. Protect participant privacy, document consent and translate findings into design or material actions with an owner and verification plan.","caution":"Do not generalise from a small convenience sample or treat subjective comfort ratings as laboratory performance measurements. Separate participant opinion from observed defects and measured results.","deep":"A wear trial should test a defined hypothesis, such as whether a fabric restricts shoulder movement, whether a waistband retains fit during activity, or whether a lining creates excessive heat or friction. Select participants who represent the intended user and relevant size range; document any limitations in the sample. Standardise garment conditioning, sizing, activity sequence, exposure duration and environmental conditions as far as practical. Collect baseline fit observations and use consistent rating scales for comfort, mobility, thermal sensation, moisture, pressure and irritation. Ask participants to identify the location, timing and activity associated with each issue. Inspect garments before and after use for seam opening, pilling, abrasion, distortion, print cracking, trim failure and dimensional change. Where claims depend on measurable properties, pair user feedback with suitable laboratory or field measurements. Analyse patterns by size, activity, wearer and material lot rather than relying only on an average score. Obtain informed consent, minimise personal data and define how records will be stored. The final report should distinguish observed facts, participant perceptions and interpretation, then specify design changes, additional tests or limits on the conclusion.","cat":"Design, Compatibility and Validation","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Wear Trials and User Evaluation\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Wear Trials and User Evaluation</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Wear Trials and User Evaluation</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material analysis and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Participant</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Activity</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Observation</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Analysis</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"25":{"title":"Life Cycle Assessment and Environmental Impacts","overview":"Life cycle assessment (LCA) evaluates environmental impacts across defined stages of a product system, from raw-material extraction or cultivation through processing, garment manufacture, use and end-of-life. Results depend on the functional unit, system boundary, allocation rules, data quality and impact categories selected. A fibre cannot be ranked as environmentally preferable from its name or one isolated indicator. Meaningful comparison requires equivalent product function and transparent assumptions.","method":"Define the product function and functional unit, such as a specified garment service or number of wears, before comparing materials. Set the system boundary and identify included stages, geography, time period, allocation rules and impact categories. Use traceable primary or secondary data, document gaps and assumptions, and compare alternatives on an equivalent basis. Check sensitivity to use-phase, durability, washing, transport and end-of-life assumptions before communicating conclusions.","caution":"Do not compare figures with different functional units, boundaries, datasets or allocation methods as if they were directly equivalent. Avoid presenting a single impact category as a complete measure of sustainability.","deep":"An LCA result is conditional on the model used. The functional unit must describe the service being compared, not merely one kilogram of fibre if the alternatives differ in garment performance or useful life. System boundaries may include cultivation or extraction, fibre production, yarn and fabric manufacture, dyeing and finishing, garment assembly, distribution, use and end-of-life; excluded stages must be stated. Allocation is needed where processes produce co-products, recycled inputs or shared outputs, and different allocation choices can change results. Data should reflect relevant geography, technology and period. Primary supplier data can improve specificity but must be checked for completeness and consistency; generic databases may be necessary where primary data are unavailable. Evaluate impact categories such as climate change, water use, land use, eutrophication, resource use and chemical-related impacts where the method supports them. Use sensitivity analysis to test assumptions about garment lifetime, washing frequency, energy mix, transport and recycling. Interpret trade-offs rather than selecting a winner from one metric. Retain the goal and scope, inventory sources, calculation method, assumptions, data quality assessment, limitations and review status. Environmental claims should match the actual study scope and should not imply that an LCA result certifies the whole product as sustainable.","cat":"Design, Compatibility and Validation","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Life Cycle Assessment and Environmental Impacts\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Life Cycle Assessment and Environmental Impacts</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Life Cycle Assessment and Environmental Impacts</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material analysis and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Goal</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Boundary</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Data</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Interpretation</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"26":{"title":"Design for Repairability and Long Service Life","overview":"Designing for repair and long service life aims to keep a garment useful for longer by reducing avoidable failure and making common repairs practical. Material durability, seam construction, replaceable components, access to fasteners, spare-part availability and care requirements all influence whether repair is feasible. Longer life cannot be assumed from a durable fabric alone; the garment must remain functional, maintainable and acceptable to its user.","method":"Identify likely failure points from use conditions, material properties and product construction. Evaluate abrasion, seam strength, colour and dimensional stability, fastening durability and care performance as relevant. Design access for replacing buttons, zippers, elastic or panels; specify repair methods and compatible spare components. Conduct repair trials and assess the appearance, strength, time, tools and skill required. Verify the repaired garment against the relevant performance and safety requirements.","caution":"Do not describe a garment as repairable merely because it can technically be opened or sewn. Consider repair access, available materials, required skills, cost and whether the repaired product remains safe and fit for use.","deep":"Repairability should be considered during material and construction selection. A zipper that is difficult to access, a bonded seam that cannot be reopened, or a unique trim with no replacement source can make a nominally durable garment impractical to repair. Identify foreseeable failure modes by component and use case: seam opening, abrasion, hole formation, fastener breakage, elastic fatigue, coating damage or localised colour loss. Select materials and construction details that allow targeted replacement rather than requiring disposal of the whole garment. Consider seam allowance, stitch type, access openings, standardised fasteners, spare-part coding, repair instructions and availability of compatible thread, fabric or trims. Evaluate whether repair operations can be completed with common tools and realistic skills without damaging adjacent components. Test repaired areas for strength, appearance, comfort and relevant care performance; a repair that restores function but creates a sharp edge, weak seam or uncomfortable ridge is not acceptable. Assess durability under representative use and care conditions, while recognising that accelerated tests do not reproduce every real-world failure. Record expected service conditions, critical failure modes, repair procedures, replacement components, limitations and validation results. Long-life claims should be supported by evidence about the complete product system, not by the durability of one material alone.","cat":"Design, Compatibility and Validation","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Design for Repairability and Long Service Life\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Design for Repairability and Long Service Life</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Design for Repairability and Long Service Life</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material analysis and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Failure-prone detail</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Repairability</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Post-repair test</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Records</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"27":{"title":"Material Parameter Standardization and Material Specifications","overview":"A material specification is a controlled document that defines material identity, construction, properties, verification methods and acceptance conditions. It must be sufficiently precise for suppliers, merchandising, quality teams, laboratories and production teams to interpret the requirement consistently. A trade name or subjective description is not enough to control variation between lots. Only properties that genuinely require control should be specified, with a defined sampling and measurement approach.","method":"Establish fields for material code, fibre composition, construction, mass, usable width, colour, finish, performance requirements, test method and edition, tolerances, sampling plan, approved supplier and revision. Separate mandatory acceptance limits from development targets. Identify the document owner, approver, effective date and change-control route. Link the specification to approved reference samples and relevant test records.","caution":"Do not set acceptance limits without technical or customer evidence, or copy limits from a different material without checking applicability. If the test method or care conditions change, assess whether historical and new results remain comparable.","deep":"A robust Material Specification translates product needs into measurable material controls. The data set may include material code, fibre composition, yarn and fabric construction, mass per unit area, usable width, colour reference, finish, functional properties, test methods and editions, tolerances, sampling, packaging, lot traceability and approved manufacturing source. Avoid collecting fields that are not used for purchasing, inspection or process control; unnecessary requirements can create cost and ambiguity. Distinguish target values from acceptance limits and state whether limits apply to individual specimens, averages or lot-level decisions. Specify conditioning, specimen preparation, equipment or method where necessary, and identify the governing standard. Link the document to a signed reference sample, approved colour standard and supplier or mill. Apply revision control with author, approver, issue date, effective date and a record of superseded versions. Changes to construction, manufacturing site, dyeing route or finishing chemistry should trigger formal change control. Retain material and lot codes, method edition, test conditions, before-and-after results, control samples, deviations and the final disposition. Do not broaden a specification result to a different lot or process unless equivalence is demonstrated.","cat":"Material Standardization and Management","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Material Parameter Standardization and Material Specifications\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Material Parameter Standardization and Material Specifications</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Material Parameter Standardization and Material Specifications</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material analysis and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Material code</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Properties</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test method</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Revision</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"28":{"title":"Material Approval, Lab Dips and Bulk Approval","overview":"Material approval is an evidence-based sequence of decisions from development sampling to production-lot confirmation. A lab dip assesses colour under laboratory conditions, but it does not guarantee an identical bulk shade because equipment, substrate, load and processing conditions may differ. Bulk approval must consider colour, construction, performance and consistency with the signed reference standard. Measurable requirements should be separated from subjective handfeel or appearance comments.","method":"Maintain a submission log and identify each physical reference sample with a code, approval date and approver. Define the light source, viewing conditions, colour measurement method and buyer tolerance. Record recipe, dye lot, machine, dyeing and finishing conditions, and laboratory results. For bulk, sample the beginning, middle and end of rolls or follow the agreed sampling plan; retain samples and document acceptance or approved exceptions. Record deviations, approver and effective date, and reassess whenever the mill, dye lot or finish changes.","caution":"Do not use a screenshot or mobile-phone photograph as the sole colour standard. Do not compare lab dips and bulk under different light sources or viewing conditions, and do not replace a faded or untraceable standard without formal approval.","deep":"Lab-dip approval requires a defined light source, viewing environment, substrate, viewing angle and approved physical standard. Record the colour code, formulation, dyehouse, fabric base and revision, and store labelled physical standards under suitable conditions. Bulk approval must evaluate actual production material rather than relying on the lab dip alone. Take samples from the beginning, middle and end of rolls, or according to the agreed sampling plan, and assess shade variation within and between lots. Use shade bands where appropriate and identify the limits of acceptable variation. Evaluate construction and required performance as well as colour; a shade pass does not establish that shrinkage, strength, handle or finish is acceptable. If a deviation occurs, segregate affected rolls or lots and decide their use through an authorised disposition rather than uncontrolled blending. Results are comparable only when methods, conditioning, specimen state and equipment are sufficiently consistent. If a reference standard is damaged, faded or no longer traceable, replace it through a documented approval process. Retain submission records, physical standards, test reports, lot and roll identifiers, deviations, approvals and final release decisions.","cat":"Material Standardization and Management","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Material Approval, Lab Dips and Bulk Approval\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Material Approval, Lab Dips and Bulk Approval</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Material Approval, Lab Dips and Bulk Approval</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material analysis and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Lab dip</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Colour approval</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Bulk sample</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Shade band</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"29":{"title":"Material Pilot Trials and Scale-Up","overview":"Laboratory results may differ from bulk production because equipment, processing time, chemical ratios, speed, temperature and environmental conditions change with scale. A pilot trial evaluates whether a process can be transferred from a small sample to representative production equipment and load. Scale-up variables such as heat and mass transfer, mixing, flow rate and residence time mean that a laboratory recipe cannot simply be multiplied to obtain a bulk recipe.","method":"Define critical-to-quality characteristics and process variables before the pilot. Run the trial on representative equipment and load, record actual process parameters over time, and sample multiple representative positions. Measure inputs and outputs, compare laboratory, pilot and bulk results, and record equipment conditions, yield, consumption, defects and stability. Expand only when operating limits, monitoring controls and stage-gate approval criteria have been established.","caution":"Do not scale a laboratory formula linearly when equipment geometry, liquor ratio, loading or heat and mass transfer differ. One successful batch is not sufficient evidence of a repeatable, controlled scale-up.","deep":"Scale-up changes the relationship between surface area and volume, chemical quantity, heating and cooling rates, flow, mixing and residence time. A laboratory recipe may not reproduce on a bulk machine because liquor ratio, machine loading, nozzle action or heat transfer differs. A pilot should therefore record actual conditions throughout the process rather than only nominal set points. Identify critical-to-quality properties and process parameters, then design trials with representative loads and planned sampling locations. Measure material properties before and after processing using comparable methods, and evaluate uniformity across the batch. Compare laboratory, pilot and bulk outcomes while documenting equipment, operator settings, productivity, resource consumption, defects and process stability. If a pilot result is not repeatable, investigate differences in equipment, loading, water, raw materials, sampling and measurement. Establish an operating window, control points, monitoring frequency and reaction plan for deviations before authorising scale-up. Do not declare success from one acceptable run; assess repeatability and the process's ability to remain within limits. Retain trial protocols, machine records, recipes, sample locations, test results, deviations, corrective actions and the approval to move to the next scale.","cat":"Material Standardization and Management","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Material Pilot Trials and Scale-Up\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Material Pilot Trials and Scale-Up</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Material Pilot Trials and Scale-Up</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material analysis and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Lab recipe</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Pilot</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Scale-up</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Process lock</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"30":{"title":"Material Change Control and Risk Assessment","overview":"Changes to a mill, fibre composition, construction, dye, finish or process can affect fit, shade, shrinkage, strength, sewability, compliance and cost. A change that appears minor in a specification may have consequences across the bill of materials (BOM) and production route. Change control must assess the impact on the complete product and process, not simply approve a substitute sample.","method":"Raise a change request stating the reason, scope, affected styles and lots, specification differences and proposed effective date. Compare old and new parameters, assess risk by severity and likelihood or detectability, and define the samples and tests that must be repeated. Assign approval responsibilities across design, sourcing, quality, laboratory and buyer teams. Update the BOM, specification and process recipe only after approval, and trace affected orders and inventory.","caution":"Do not substitute a supposedly equivalent material based only on its trade name or a supplier's verbal assurance. Unapproved material changes must not be introduced on the production line.","deep":"Assess a proposed change across fibre composition, supplier and mill, construction, mass, colour, finish, usable width, shrinkage, functional properties, manufacturing source and process route. Compare the old and new specifications property by property, identify non-equivalent characteristics and evaluate risks to cutting, sewing, washing, appearance, durability and chemical compliance. Determine which styles, colours, sizes, purchase orders and inventory are affected. Use a structured risk assessment to consider severity, occurrence and the ability to detect a failure; the selected scoring method and decision thresholds should be defined by the organisation. Specify the evidence needed to approve the change, including replacement samples, laboratory tests, pilot trials or garment-level validation. Obtain approval from the relevant design, sourcing, quality, laboratory and buyer stakeholders before updating the BOM, specification or recipe. Control the transition between old and new stock with clear lot identification and an agreed cut-in date; prevent uncontrolled mixing. If validation fails, contain affected material, identify impacted orders and define a stop or reversion decision. Retain the change request, comparison of specifications, risk assessment, test evidence, approvals, inventory disposition, effective date and final closure record.","cat":"Material Standardization and Management","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Material Change Control and Risk Assessment\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Material Change Control and Risk Assessment</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Material Change Control and Risk Assessment</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material analysis and validation sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Change request</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Impact</text><text x=\"294\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">assessment</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Retest</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Approval</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record sample ID, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"31":{"title":"Technical Records and the Digital Material Library","cat":"Material Standardization and Management","overview":"A material library allows development teams to reuse verified information and avoid repeating approvals from the beginning. Each record must be linked to a physical sample, supplier and revision.\n\nThe subject should be understood through the relationship between the material's underlying structure, its intended application and the available technical evidence. A digital material library must be a structured, version-controlled data source, not merely a folder containing PDFs or photographs. A record should link the material code, supplier, composition, construction, colour, specification, test reports, compliance documents, physical sample and change history. Searchability depends on consistent coding rules and reliable metadata. Learners must distinguish measurable requirements from subjective assessments; where no specific test method or test conditions have been defined, a conclusion should be treated only as a development hypothesis.","method":"Standardize material codes, taxonomy, images, swatch locations, specifications, test reports, suppliers, cost, lead time, certification scope and change history. Define who may create, approve and retire a code.\n\nThe workflow should begin by locking the specification revision and identifying the representative sample. Design the taxonomy and mandatory fields; assign permissions for creating, editing and approving records; and retain the revision, effective date, original files and links to product codes. Establish checks for duplicate codes, expired documents and materials that are no longer approved. Test whether a user can move from a technical requirement to the correct sample, report and supplier. Record every deviation from the reference sample, the approver and the effective date. A record should advance only when evidence demonstrates that the agreed criteria have been met.","caution":"Do not allow multiple specification versions to be marked as current at the same time.\n\nResults cannot be compared reliably when test methods, conditioning conditions, sample states or equipment differ. Never overwrite a revision that was used for a previous order. Records missing a source, date or approver must be marked as unverified; digital data must be checked against the physical sample and supplier documentation. If evidence is incomplete, state the limitation and request further testing rather than extending the conclusion beyond what the data supports.","deep":"A digital material library requires a unique identifier for each material, linked to its supplier, mill, composition, construction, colour, specification, test reports, certificates and physical sample. Attribute-based search helps development teams reuse validated materials, but each record must display its status: under development, approved, restricted or discontinued. Control editing rights, revision history and expired documents. Avoid multiple unsynchronized local copies; the digital record must be traceable to the actual lot and revision used.\n\nTechnical analysis and decision-making: The digital material library must be a structured, version-controlled data source, not merely a folder containing PDFs or photographs. Records should link the material code, supplier, composition, construction, colour, specification, test reports, compliance documents, physical sample and change history. Searchability depends on consistent coding rules and reliable metadata. Control point: define the taxonomy and mandatory fields; assign create, edit and approval permissions; retain revisions, effective dates, original files and links to product codes. Establish checks for duplicate codes, expired certificates and materials no longer approved. Test retrieval from a technical requirement to the correct sample, report and supplier. Investigation scenario: never overwrite a revision used for a previous order. Records missing a source, date or approver must be marked unverified, and digital data must be checked against the physical sample and supplier documentation. At minimum, retain the material/lot code, method revision, conditioning details, before-and-after results, reference sample, deviations and final decision.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Technical Records and the Digital Material Library\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Technical Records and the Digital Material Library</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Technical Records and the Digital Material Library</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Validation Sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Unique Identifier</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Digital Record</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Revision</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Status</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"32":{"title":"Practical Case: Selecting Material for a Running Shirt","cat":"Material Development and Validation Practice","overview":"A brief calls for a lightweight running shirt for hot outdoor conditions. The garment should dry relatively quickly, cling less when the wearer sweats and retain its shape after washing. The development team is considering three knitted-fabric options. The objective is to translate the brief into criteria, compare the options and identify what must be tested before a final selection.","method":"Do not select a fabric on the basis of a single property. First define the use environment, activity level, washing frequency, desired wearing comfort and price ceiling. Then build a weighted decision matrix agreed by the project team. The scores below are simulated data for practising the decision-making process only.","caution":"The illustrative scores are not supplier test data and do not demonstrate that any option meets the actual requirements. Before placing a bulk order, obtain samples matching the intended article code, colour, finish and production lot.","deep":"A running-shirt case should begin with the brief: activity intensity, climate, layering, fit, washing frequency and target price. Identify whether the torso, underarm, shoulder and side panels have different requirements; consider combining materials if one construction cannot meet every functional need. Build a shortlist using actual data for GSM, stretch, recovery, wicking, drying, abrasion resistance and colourfastness to perspiration. Make a garment prototype, assess seam growth and conduct a wear trial. The final decision must balance performance, supply capability, colour consistency and cost.","practice":"<h3>3. Simulated Data for Comparison</h3>\n<table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Fabric A</th><th>Fabric B</th><th>Fabric C</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Construction</td><td>Polyester jersey</td><td>Polyester–elastane jersey</td><td>Polyester double knit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sample GSM</td><td>125</td><td>145</td><td>170</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hand feel from internal assessment</td><td>Dry, slightly rough</td><td>Soft, stretchy</td><td>Thick, stable</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Preliminary quoted price</td><td>Low</td><td>Medium</td><td>High</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Missing data</td><td>Wicking, pilling, dimensional stability</td><td>Recovery after washing, pilling, shade</td><td>Air permeability, drying, weight after washing</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p class=\"small\">Descriptions and price levels are assumptions for this exercise, not market specifications or test results.</p>\n<h3>4. Decision-Making Approach</h3>\n<ol><li>Eliminate options that fail mandatory requirements once test data are available.</li><li>Score the remaining options using agreed weights for wearing comfort, moisture management, dimensional stability after washing, durability, manufacturability and price.</li><li>Select samples for laboratory testing; retain reference samples and code each sample to prevent mix-ups.</li><li>Confirm the material only after test results, wear-trial findings, costing and supply capability have all been considered.</li></ol>\n<h3>5. Exercise</h3><p>Name three tests that should be prioritized for a running shirt and explain why. Then identify which data in the table are insufficient to conclude that Fabric B is better than Fabric A.</p>","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Practical Case: Selecting Material for a Running Shirt\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Practical Case: Selecting Material for a Running Shirt</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Practical Case: Selecting Material for a Running Shirt</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Validation Sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Running-Shirt Brief</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Shortlist</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Prototype Sewing</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Wear Trial</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"33":{"title":"Test Planning and Functional Claim Matrix","cat":"Material Development and Validation Practice","overview":"A claim such as quick-dry, moisture management, anti-odor or UV protection must be connected to the material mechanism, test method and intended scope of use. A claim matrix prevents a product team from using the result of one test to support a broader statement than the evidence allows.","method":"For each claim, record the exact proposed wording, the property to be demonstrated, the test method accepted by the buyer or laboratory, the test specimen, preconditioning conditions, approval criteria and limits on interpretation. If the claim must remain valid after washing, specify the required number of cycles and the designated washing method.","caution":"Do not invent pass/fail thresholds or treat the name of a test method as proof of compliance. Criteria must come from the specification, market requirements, buyer or an authorized compliance specialist.","deep":"A claim matrix should include the claim, area of application, material mechanism, test method, source of the acceptance criteria, specimen and lot, result, testing organization and approval status. For example, a “quick-dry” claim requires a defined drying measurement method; “UV protection” requires the fabric configuration, colour, moisture condition and post-wash state to be specified when relevant. Plan testing before sample development so that the selected test can actually substantiate the intended claim. If results support only a limited set of conditions, restrict product communication to that evidence-based scope.","practice":"<h3>3. Example of a Claim Matrix</h3>\n<table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Proposed Claim</th><th>Property to Demonstrate</th><th>Records to Retain</th><th>Limits on Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Quick-dry</td><td>Moisture reduction rate or drying time under the selected method</td><td>Method, environmental conditions, specimen state and time-series data</td><td>Drying results alone do not establish moisture absorption or breathability</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Moisture wicking</td><td>Liquid spreading in the specified direction</td><td>Test direction, liquid quantity, time and images or measurements of the spread area</td><td>Not interchangeable with drying rate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anti-odor</td><td>Effectiveness against the specified target under defined test conditions</td><td>Method, test organism or target where applicable, washing conditions and laboratory report</td><td>A limited result must not be expanded into a broad antimicrobial claim</td></tr>\n<tr><td>UV protection</td><td>UV blocking capability of the tested fabric construction</td><td>Method, dry/wet state, colour, stretch and post-wash condition where relevant</td><td>A result for one specimen must not be applied to every colour and construction</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>4. Test Request Form</h3>\n<ul><li>Material code, revision, colour, lot and sample source.</li><li>Intended use and the claim to be substantiated.</li><li>Method and version specified by the authorized party.</li><li>Preconditioning, number of specimens, cutting direction and test conditions.</li><li>Acceptance criteria, approver and disposition of nonconforming results.</li></ul>\n<h3>5. Exercise</h3><p>Marketing wants to state “dries quickly and provides long-lasting odor resistance.” Separate this into two claims, list the evidence required for each and identify at least two limitations that should appear in the product documentation.</p>","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Test Planning and Functional Claim Matrix\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Test Planning and Functional Claim Matrix</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Test Planning and Functional Claim Matrix</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Validation Sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Claim</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Method</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Result</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Scope Approval</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"34":{"title":"Practical Exercise: Preparing a Material Specification and Sample Approval Record","cat":"Material Standardization and Management Practice","overview":"The Material Specification is the common reference point for design, sourcing, suppliers, laboratories, QA and the factory. A material code must identify its construction, composition, colour, width, mass, finish, tests, revision and approval status.","method":"Create a sample record with a unique material code. Record the supplier and manufacturing mill, article code, declared composition, construction, GSM and width as required by the project, colour and colour code, finish, test methods, attachments, sampling date, revision and approver. Clearly distinguish supplier-declared parameters from independently verified test results.","caution":"Do not allow a specification file without a revision to be used for both development samples and bulk production. When the mill, finishing formulation, construction or raw-material source changes, reassess the scope of impact.","deep":"A practical specification should include the material code, revision, effective date, construction description, composition, width, GSM, colour, finish, function, test methods, criteria, tolerances, packaging and lot labelling. The sample approval record should link the physical swatch, reference photographs, laboratory reports, comments, approver and status. Every change should have a change request stating the reason, impact assessment, replacement sample and approval. When a new revision is released, mark the previous version as obsolete and notify purchasing, QA, warehouse, cutting and the supplier.","practice":"<h3>3. Example Structure of a Material Specification</h3>\n<table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Data Field</th><th>Example Entry</th><th>Control</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Material ID / Revision</td><td>MAT-TS-014 / Rev 03</td><td>Illustrative code; do not reuse a code for a different material</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Supplier / Mill / Article</td><td>Record the legal entity, mill and article code</td><td>Distinguish the seller from the actual manufacturing site</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fibre Content / Construction</td><td>Record declared composition and woven or knitted construction</td><td>Identify whether the data are supplier-declared or laboratory-verified</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colour / Finish</td><td>Colour code, colour name and finish type</td><td>Link the lab dip, bulk standard and applicable scope</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Physical Properties</td><td>GSM, usable width and thickness where required</td><td>Record units, method, conditions and approved tolerances</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Testing / Approval</td><td>List of tests, reports and reference samples</td><td>Record status, date and approver</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>4. Checks Before Release</h3>\n<ul><li>No two files share the same code while carrying different revisions.</li><li>The reference sample is labelled with its date, colour, lot and approval signature or status.</li><li>Test reports can be traced back to the correct specimen.</li><li>The factory and sourcing team receive the released revision.</li><li>Superseded versions are clearly marked and cannot be selected inadvertently.</li></ul>\n<h3>5. Exercise</h3><p>The supplier submits Specification Rev 02, QA holds a Rev 01 sample, and the bulk purchase order lists the material code without a revision. State the steps required before production can be confirmed.</p>","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Practical Exercise: Preparing a Material Specification and Sample Approval Record\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Practical Exercise: Preparing a Material Specification and Sample Approval Record</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Practical Exercise: Preparing a Material Specification and Sample Approval Record</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Validation Sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Specification</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Reference Sample</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test Report</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Release</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"35":{"title":"Practical Case: Assessing a Supplier or Fabric-Construction Change","cat":"Material Standardization and Management Practice","overview":"A supplier proposes changing the yarn source and adjusting the fabric construction because of a raw-material shortage. Price and delivery lead time may improve, but the change could affect GSM, shade, hand feel, shrinkage, strength, performance after washing and production-line capability.","method":"Raise a coded change request with a revision; describe exactly what will change, why, and which lots and orders are affected. Compare the old and new specifications, identify affected properties, select tests and reference specimens, conduct the technical assessment, update costing and lead time, and obtain approval from the relevant stakeholders before releasing a new revision.","caution":"Do not accept a substitution solely on the basis of a supplier's letter stating that the materials are equivalent. The level of testing must reflect risk, quality history, product function and customer requirements.","deep":"Assess a new supplier or construction according to risk, not quotation price alone. Verify equipment capability, process controls, lot control, laboratory capability, traceability and quality history. Prepare a parameter-by-parameter comparison of the current and proposed materials, obtain samples from the intended production source, and conduct functional testing and garment trials. Consider effects on consumption, marker efficiency, needles and thread, sewing speed, finishing and care requirements. Introduce the change under control by style and lot, then monitor the first production order.","practice":"<h3>3. Change Impact Assessment Matrix</h3>\n<table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Question to Verify</th><th>Evidence / Action</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Composition and Yarn</td><td>Have the fibre source, blend ratio or yarn structure changed?</td><td>Declaration and composition test results where required</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Appearance and Parameters</td><td>Have GSM, width, thickness, density or shade changed?</td><td>Specification comparison, reference samples and measurement results</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Performance</td><td>Could shrinkage, strength, pilling, colour, wicking or coating be affected?</td><td>Risk-based test plan and result reports</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Manufacturing</td><td>Do spreading, cutting, needles, seams, bonding or finishing need adjustment?</td><td>Factory trial, defect records and productivity observations</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Commercial and Compliance</td><td>Have price, lead time, documents, claims or buyer requirements changed?</td><td>Updated costing, compliance documents and relevant approvals</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>4. Change Request Workflow</h3>\n<p>Propose the change → classify the risk level → identify affected orders and lots → plan samples and testing → conduct a factory assessment if required → obtain cross-functional approval → update the specification and revision → notify all points of use → monitor the first lot.</p>\n<h3>5. Exercise</h3><p>The new fabric has the same fibre composition and GSM as the previous sample, but it comes from a different weaving mill and uses a different finishing formulation. Why is it not yet possible to conclude that the two materials are equivalent? Prepare a minimum checklist based on the product and the claims currently in use.</p>","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase13-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Practical Case: Assessing a Supplier or Fabric-Construction Change\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Practical Case: Assessing a Supplier or Fabric-Construction Change</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Practical Case: Assessing a Supplier or Fabric-Construction Change</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Material Analysis and Validation Sequence</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Supplier</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Production Sample</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Testing</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf4f7\" stroke=\"#4f7787\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Transition</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the sample code, test conditions, results and relevant revision.</text></svg></figure>"}},"categories":{"Liên kết với thiết kế & phát triển sản phẩm":"Links to Design and Product Development","Phát triển sản phẩm và lựa chọn vật liệu":"Product Development and Material Selection","Thử nghiệm và hiệu năng vật liệu":"Material Testing and Performance","Vật liệu chức năng và bền vững":"Functional and Sustainable Materials","Thiết kế, tương thích và xác nhận":"Design, Compatibility and Validation","Tiêu chuẩn hóa và quản trị vật liệu":"Material Standardization and Management","Thực hành phát triển và xác nhận vật liệu":"Material Development and Validation Practice","Thực hành tiêu chuẩn hóa và quản trị vật liệu":"Material Standardization and Management Practice"},"ui":{"crumb":"← Garment Academy Library","nav_home":"HOME","nav_apps":"🛠️ APPS","nav_apps_sub":"Explore Apps","nav_games":"🎮 GAMES","nav_games_sub":"Game Hub","nav_tools":"🧰 TOOLS","nav_tools_sub":"Explore Tools","nav_ai":"✨ AI","nav_ai_sub":"AI Hub","nav_lottery":"🎟️ LOTTERY","nav_market":"🛍️ MARKET","nav_news":"📰 NEWS","nav_academy":"📚 GARMENT ACADEMY","academy_small":"TEXTILE AND GARMENT KNOWLEDGE LIBRARY","academy_nav_design":"Design and Products","academy_nav_materials":"Materials and Textiles","academy_nav_manufacturing":"Manufacturing","academy_nav_quality":"Quality","academy_nav_business":"Management and Trade","academy_nav_dictionary":"Dictionary","eyebrow":"GARMENT ACADEMY · KNOWLEDGE LIBRARY","page_title":"Advanced Materials, Performance and Material Management","intro":"From end-use requirements to fabric construction, performance testing, material validation, change management and traceability. For product development, sourcing, textile technologists, QA and merchandising professionals.","related_title":"Related content:","related":"Two overview topics on the product development process and translating user needs into technical requirements are covered in greater detail in","related_design":"Design and Product Development","related_mid":"This page focuses on material properties, performance, selection, testing and material management. Detailed test methods and laboratory operations are expanded in","related_lab":"Textile Testing Laboratory","flow_title":"Material Validation Workflow","flow_sub":"Lab samples, approved samples and bulk production must be linked through material codes, revisions and test records.","flow_a1":"End-use","flow_a2":"Requirements","flow_b1":"Material","flow_b2":"Selection","flow_c1":"Lab Testing","flow_c2":"and Wear Trial","flow_d1":"Specification","flow_d2":"/ Sample Approval","flow_e1":"Pilot","flow_e2":"and Scale-up","flow_f1":"Bulk","flow_f2":"Approval","flow_loop":"Every material change must be reassessed and reapproved according to risk.","search":"Search lessons: GSM, pilling, wicking, laminate, RSL…","all_categories":"All content groups","lesson_nav":"Lesson list","empty":"No matching lessons found.","section1":"1. Principles and Scope","section2":"2. Implementation","section3":"3. Technical Analysis and Application","caution_label":"Control Point","footer_tagline":"A little of everything. All worth using.","footer_start":"GET STARTED","footer_home":"Home","footer_apps":"Apps","footer_tools":"Tools","footer_ai":"AI","footer_games":"Games","footer_academy":"ACADEMY","footer_dictionary":"English–Vietnamese–Chinese Dictionary","footer_supply":"Supply Chain and Trade","footer_digital":"Digital Transformation and Data","footer_appnho":"APPNHO","footer_privacy":"Privacy Policy","footer_terms":"Terms of Use","footer_contact":"Contact","footer_about":"About AppNho","footer_bottom":"Technical specifications must be checked against the approved tech pack, standards and requirements for each product.","menu_label":"Open menu","appnho_nav_label":"AppNho navigation","academy_nav_label":"Academy navigation","academy_groups_label":"Six knowledge groups","flow_aria":"Material validation workflow","pending":"This lesson is not yet available in English."}};

